WEBVTT

00:00:00.198 --> 00:00:04.460
Reconnaissance is the information-gathering stage that may happen before visible activity

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.832
inside the organization.

00:00:05.832 --> 00:00:09.020
A defender may never see all of it, which is why preparation matters.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.880
The defensive question is not how to perform reconnaissance;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.850
it is what useful information the organization is exposing and how

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.515
that exposure can be reduced.

00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:19.845
Public-facing systems, stale DNS records, forgotten test services,

00:00:19.845 --> 00:00:22.860
exposed contact information, unmanaged cloud assets,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.595
and vague ownership all make later analysis harder.

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:29.075
Good defensive preparation starts with asset inventory, ownership records,

00:00:29.075 --> 00:00:32.240
external exposure review, patch and configuration hygiene,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.580
and monitoring for unusual probing against systems the organization cares about.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.570
Analysts should also understand what normal internet noise looks like so they can

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.495
distinguish routine scanning from activity that deserves

00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:43.476
closer review.

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:46.680
Reconnaissance is an early-stage concept, but the defensive work is ongoing.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.560
Keep inventories current, remove unnecessary exposure,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.840
validate who owns internet-facing services,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.600
and make sure logs exist before an incident begins.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.805
When later evidence appears,

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:59.680
that preparation helps the team answer practical questions quickly: what was exposed,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.635
what changed, what was targeted, and which controls were in place.

00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:06.200
Preparation also makes later incident notes more reliable.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.170
If the team already knows which assets are exposed, who owns them,

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.700
and which logs are available,

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.080
analysts spend less time reconstructing basic context and more time understanding whether

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.350
the current activity represents meaningful risk.
